The code statements that lead to the bug are:
unsigned int block_offset;
unsigned int delta;
Reason:
These 32-bit unsigned int variables are used to store and compute DMA addresses. When the IOMMU creates a single large block spanning multiple SG entries with total size ≥ 4G, arithmetic like `BIT_ULL(biter->__pg_bit) - block_offset` overflows the 32-bit range, producing incorrect `delta` values and hence wrong DMA addresses for subsequent blocks.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
dma_addr_t block_offset;
dma_addr_t delta;
Reason:
Changing the types to `dma_addr_t` (typically 64-bit) ensures that the block offset and delta can hold the full range of DMA addresses, preventing overflow when dealing with block sizes ≥ 4G and correctly reassembling split SG entries.

